"Textbook: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §§{#}–{#} and {#}–{#} (rings and arcs of charge in §{#}). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Dipole; Like pair; +{#} and −{#}; Triangle; Quadrupole; Rod; Ring; Disk; Arc; Ring in the map.",
"Hover over the field, tap to place the probe, or press P on the field and use the arrow keys. The probe sits on a {#} cm grid. Each charge is modeled as a uniformly charged ball {#} mm in radius, inside its drawn disc; outside the ball V and E are exact point charge values.",
"The field of this object alone (other charges left out), compared with the formulas of OpenStax section {#} at the probe. Each formula is exact only where its note says; elsewhere it is a limit. Select another rod, ring or disk to compare it.",
"Capture stores the probe position and the values shown above, rounded as displayed, with the charges that produced them (listed in the CSV header). Cells can be edited; rows stay through setup changes until Clear. Press C on the field to capture.",
"Blind hides the field lines, the equipotentials, the potential shading, the 3D landscape and the field map data table. The charges, the grid and the probe stay, so students map the field from probe readings.",
"In 3D, height is the potential: hills at positive charges, wells at negative ones. Heights are compressed near each charge, where V grows without bound.",
"Along a line graphs V and the part of E along a line through the probe (or along a ring’s or disk’s axis, an arc’s line of symmetry or a rod’s bisector), with −ΔV/Δs worked out from V alone as dots.",
"A ring or disk stands across the map with its axis in the map; an arc lies in the map, and an arc of {#}° is a ring lying in the map. Their V and E are the exact integrals of k dq/r.",
"Use electric field and potential measurements to test the distance laws for a charge, investigate how two charges combine, and test the textbook results for a ring and an arc of charge.",
"Outside a small spherical charge, the field and potential are those of a point charge (§§{#}–{#} and {#}):",
"\\[\\mathbf E=\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0r^2}\\hat{\\mathbf r},\\qquad V=\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0r}.\\]",
"Fields add as vectors and potentials add as signed numbers. Potential differences determine the field (§{#}):",
"\\[E_x=-\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial x},\\qquad E_y=-\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial y}.\\]",
"A small displacement along an equipotential has no component along the field (§{#}).",
"<strong>A ring and an arc of charge (§{#}).</strong> Adding up \\(k\\,dq/r^2\\) over a ring of radius \\(R\\) and charge \\(Q\\) gives, at a distance \\(z\\) along its axis,",
"\\[E_z=\\frac{kQz}{\\left(z^2+R^2\\right)^{3/2}},\\qquad V=\\frac{kQ}{\\sqrt{z^2+R^2}}.\\]",
"At the center of an arc of radius \\(R\\) that spans an angle \\(\\theta\\) (in radians) with \\(\\lambda = Q/(R\\theta)\\), every bit of charge is the same distance \\(R\\) away, and the sideways parts of the field cancel in pairs:",
"\\[E=\\frac{2k\\lambda\\sin(\\theta/2)}{R},\\qquad V=\\frac{kQ}{R}.\\]",
"<strong>Potential energy of a set of charges (§{#}).</strong> Bringing a charge \\(q\\) from far away to a point where the other charges make a potential \\(V\\) takes work \\(W = qV\\), and that work is stored as electric potential energy. Assembling charges one at a time therefore stores",
"\\[U = \\sum_{\\text{pairs}}\\frac{q_iq_j}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 r_{ij}},\\]",
"one term for each pair, counted once. \\(U\\) is negative when attraction wins: then work must be done to pull the charges apart. The demo shows \\(U\\) for point charges in microjoules (\\(1\\ \\mu\\mathrm J = 10^{-6}\\) J).",
"Predict how \\(E\\) and \\(V\\) change if the distance from one charge doubles.",
"Can \\(V\\) be zero where the electric field is nonzero? Sketch an example.",
